WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.403
Last time, we ended with a
wish: one ruler for risk,

00:00:03.443 --> 00:00:08.627
where everything we know about a
patient turns into a single probability.

00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:11.010
Here's the obvious way to build it.

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:16.157
Start a typical patient at 20%, then
add points for each risk factor.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:24.043
Age 81: plus 25. Diabetes: plus 30. A
third hospital stay this year: plus 35.

00:00:24.083 --> 00:00:26.219
That's 110%.

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:31.644
A probability can't do that. Adding points
was the wrong move, on the wrong ruler.

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:35.354
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

00:00:35.394 --> 00:00:40.246
Today: odds, and the curve that
turns any patient into a probability.

00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:43.934
Let's try a different ruler.

00:00:43.974 --> 00:00:47.961
Take ten patients who each have
a 20% chance of coming back.

00:00:48.001 --> 00:00:50.767
On average, two do, and eight don't.

00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:53.736
Probability compares the two with all ten.

00:00:53.776 --> 00:00:57.774
Odds compare the two with the
eight: one to four, or a quarter.

00:00:57.917 --> 00:01:01.760
At 50%, it's five against five: odds of one.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.350
At 80%, eight against two: odds of four.

00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:14.211
And as the probability creeps toward 100%,
the odds just keep running: 9, 99, 999.

00:01:14.251 --> 00:01:16.308
This ruler has no ceiling.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.523
But it's a lopsided ruler.

00:01:19.563 --> 00:01:23.529
Every patient below fifty-fifty
is squeezed between zero and one,

00:01:23.569 --> 00:01:27.123
while everyone above gets
all the room in the world.

00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:29.254
The cure is a logarithm.

00:01:29.294 --> 00:01:33.158
On a log scale, every doubling
of the odds is one equal step:

00:01:33.198 --> 00:01:35.724
a quarter, a half, one, two, four.

00:01:35.883 --> 00:01:39.662
This is the log-odds ruler.
Fifty-fifty sits at zero.

00:01:39.702 --> 00:01:47.177
20% is about minus 1.4, and 80%
is plus 1.4: perfectly symmetric.

00:01:47.317 --> 00:01:53.342
And it runs forever in both directions. No
floor, no ceiling, nothing to bump into.

00:01:54.633 --> 00:01:59.841
Here's why that matters. On the log-odds
ruler, a risk factor is simply a step.

00:01:59.881 --> 00:02:05.458
Take one that multiplies the odds
by four: a step of 1.4 to the right.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.894
Now give that same step to
four different patients.

00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:12.851
On the probability ruler, 5% becomes 17%.

00:02:12.891 --> 00:02:18.322
20% becomes 50%. 50% becomes
80%. And 80% becomes 94%.

00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:22.928
Same step, very different jumps: big
in the middle, small near the ends.

00:02:22.968 --> 00:02:25.750
And nobody ever goes past one hundred.

00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:29.918
Now picture a risk factor
that comes in amounts,

00:02:29.958 --> 00:02:32.796
like the number of earlier hospital stays.

00:02:32.836 --> 00:02:37.324
Suppose each extra stay is one
more equal step in log-odds.

00:02:37.467 --> 00:02:40.116
On this scale, the pattern is a straight line.

00:02:40.156 --> 00:02:43.897
Its slope is the size of the
step, and we'll call it beta.

00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:47.321
Now switch the vertical
axis back to probability.

00:02:47.361 --> 00:02:50.296
The line bends into an S: steep in the middle,

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:53.983
flat at both ends, and
always between zero and one.

00:02:54.133 --> 00:02:56.979
How steep it is depends on where you stand.

00:02:57.019 --> 00:03:00.536
The steepness is proportional
to p times one minus p:

00:03:00.576 --> 00:03:04.546
greatest at fifty-fifty,
fading to nothing at the ends.

00:03:04.586 --> 00:03:08.307
Keep that in your pocket. It
comes back when we fit the model.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:11.141
Turn beta up, and the S gets steeper.

00:03:11.181 --> 00:03:14.923
Shift the starting point, and
the whole curve slides sideways.

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:20.010
Let's fit one of these
by hand. Remember Cedar?

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:25.776
Ten lower-risk patients, 2 readmitted.
Ten higher-risk patients, 6 readmitted.

00:03:25.917 --> 00:03:30.406
So the odds are 2 to 8, a quarter;
and 6 to 4, one and a half.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:34.684
Divide one by the other, and
you get an odds ratio of six.

00:03:34.724 --> 00:03:38.601
At Cedar, higher risk
multiplies the odds by six.

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:45.366
On the log-odds ruler, that's a step
of 1.79, starting from minus 1.39.

00:03:45.406 --> 00:03:49.255
Those two numbers are a complete
logistic model of Cedar.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:53.797
One caution. Six times the
odds is not six times the risk.

00:03:53.837 --> 00:03:58.603
Cedar's rate went from 20%
to 60%: three times, not six.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.361
Now, back to our patient. This
time, we add on the right ruler.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:10.227
Each risk factor is a step in log-odds, and
the steps simply stack, one after another.

00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:13.128
And the probability? 94%.

00:04:13.168 --> 00:04:15.438
High, but still a probability.

00:04:15.583 --> 00:04:20.556
From now on, every chip carries its
probability with it, as a shaded slice.

00:04:20.596 --> 00:04:25.042
A sliver for a low-risk patient;
nearly the whole disc for this one.

00:04:26.433 --> 00:04:27.929
Let's gather it up.

00:04:27.969 --> 00:04:31.406
Odds compare the patients who
come back with those who don't.

00:04:31.446 --> 00:04:34.585
Log-odds is the ruler where risk factors add.

00:04:34.625 --> 00:04:37.898
And the S-curve carries the
sum back to a probability.

00:04:38.033 --> 00:04:40.958
So now every patient has a probability.

00:04:40.998 --> 00:04:44.569
Add them up across a clinic, and
you get a number of patients:

00:04:44.609 --> 00:04:46.938
the readmissions we should expect.

00:04:47.083 --> 00:04:51.211
Next time, we'll set that number
beside what actually happened.

00:04:51.251 --> 00:04:53.321
Observed, versus expected.

00:04:53.467 --> 00:04:57.572
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
